  "body": {
    "id": "lem:paired-channel-factorization",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every \\(P\\in\\mathcal M\\), \\(x\\in\\mathcal X\\), and \\((s,t)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\), \\(F_x(s,t)=p_x\\varphi_{f,x}(s+t)\\varphi_1(s)\\varphi_2(t)\\) and \\(G_x(s,t)=p_x\\varphi_{q,x}(s+t)\\varphi_1(s)\\varphi_2(t)\\); moreover \\(\\inf_{|u|\\le\\nu_*}|\\varphi_j(u)|\\ge1/2\\) for \\(j\\in\\{1,2\\}\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(P\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal M\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal X\\)",
      "\\(F_x\\)",
      "\\(G_x\\)",
      "\\(p_x\\)",
      "\\(\\varphi_{f,x}\\)",
      "\\(\\varphi_{q,x}\\)",
      "\\(\\varphi_1\\)",
      "\\(\\varphi_2\\)",
      "\\(\\nu_*\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:model-class",
      "def:observable-channels",
      "ass:error-latent-independence",
      "ass:error-coordinate-independence",
      "ass:error-second-moment"
    ],
    "justification": "The common nuisance factor makes the probability and marked channels comparable on one protected cube, while the moment bound derives protection rather than assuming it.",
    "gap": "Capitao-Miniconi, Gassiat, and Lehéricy derive the unmarked factorization; the signed outcome-marked factorization and its joint use for a causal quotient are not part of their result.",
    "consumer": "The paired factorization is the input to the sharp inverse modulus and to every computable profile restriction.",
    "proof_tex": "Fix \\(P\\in\\mathcal M\\), \\(x\\in\\mathcal X\\), and \\((s,t)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\).  Using \\(S_j=A+U_j\\), first condition on \\((A,X,Y)\\).  Error--latent independence and coordinate independence give\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nF_x(s,t)\n&=E_P\\!\\left[\\mathbf 1\\{X=x\\}e^{i(s+t)A}\n E_P(e^{isU_1})E_P(e^{itU_2})\\right]\\\\\n&=p_x\\varphi_{f,x}(s+t)\\varphi_1(s)\\varphi_2(t).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe same conditioning, now retaining the bounded mark \\(Y\\), gives\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nG_x(s,t)\n&=E_P\\!\\left[\\mathbf 1\\{X=x\\}Y e^{i(s+t)A}\\right]\n  \\varphi_1(s)\\varphi_2(t)\\\\\n&=p_x\\left\\{\\int_{\\mathcal A}e^{i(s+t)a}m_x(a)f_x(a)\\,da\\right\\}\n  \\varphi_1(s)\\varphi_2(t)\\\\\n&=p_x\\varphi_{q,x}(s+t)\\varphi_1(s)\\varphi_2(t).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nAll conditional integrals exist because consistency and bounded potential outcomes imply \\(|Y|\\le B_Y\\) almost surely.  Finally, \\(|e^{iv}-1|\\le|v|\\), Cauchy--Schwarz, and the second-moment assumption imply, for \\(|u|\\le\\nu_*\\),\n\\[\n |\\varphi_j(u)|\\ge1-E_P|e^{iuU_j}-1|\n \\ge1-|u|E_P|U_j|\n \\ge1-|u|\\sqrt{M_e}\\ge\\frac12.\n\\]\nThis proves both factorizations and the protected-frequency bound.",
